\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Corrosion represents one of the major equipment integrity challenges in the oil and gas industry,
 and inhibitor injection is one of the primary methods used to combat it.
 Selecting an inhibitor and its dosage depends on the joint analysis of variables such as pH,
 C02 and H2S concentrations,
 temperature,
 pressure,
 salinity,
 and flow rate,
 which presents a significant challenge for students in training.
 This work presents the design and implementation of educational software in C++,
 using object-oriented programming,
 for corrosion analysis and inhibitor class selection in oil production systems.
 Based on scenario data,
 the program validates inputs,
 estimates the corrosion rate using a simple empirical model,
 classifies its type and severity,
 selects the inhibitor class according to sequential criteria,
 estimates dosage in ppm,
 and presents the results both numerically and graphically.
 The system was modeled in UML (use case,
 package,
 class,
 sequence,
 state machine,
 and activity diagrams) and organized into five packages,
 featuring a polymorphic inhibitor hierarchy that can be extended.
 Validation is performed in a controlled environment through test scenarios compared against independent calculations and literature trends.
 The software serves a didactic and educational purpose and was developed as an auxiliary tool for the course Fundamentals of Corrosion Applied to the Oil Industry (LEP01550).
\lang brazilian

